linker).[11] This initial MOF was prepared from the solvothermal reaction, exhibiting uniform octahedral shape and narrow size distribution (Figure S1 in the Supporting Information). Distinct from previous studies, this preparation of Mo-based hybrid used the commercially available $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ (Figure S2) which is easily obtained. Under the CVD condition (Figure S3), the ultrafine pores of UiO-66 $(0.6\mathrm{nm})$ capture the vaporized $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ which sublimes above $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S4).[12] Afterwards, the adsorbed $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ species are reduced by the corresponding confined carburization reaction to produce small $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ nanocrystallites. The carburization process effectively avoided the aggregation of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ nanoparticles (NPs), which is different from the previously reported one-pot strategy.[13] Meanwhile, the SBUs are transferred in situ into the $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ due to the pyrolysis of carboxylate groups. Furthermore, the inert $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ NPs were removed by HF solution to expand the surface reactive area. Finally, the $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ NPs are converted into MoP at $850^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ by $\mathrm{PH}_{3}$ which was generated in situ from the thermal decomposition of $(\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{2}\mathrm{HPO_{4}}$ .[6b]
As shown in Figure S5, $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ nanoparticles within $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}@{\mathrm C}$ both distributed homogeneously in the nano-octahedral carbon and retained their monodispersity. As verified by the missing peaks from $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) spectra, the inert $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ nanoparticles were readily etched by HF solution, leaving the monoclinic phase of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ . Thus, a porous nanooctahedral carbon composed of small $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ nanocrystals with a diameter of $3\mathrm{nm}$ was obtained, which was defined as $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}@\mathbf{PC}$ (Figure 1a and Figure S6). The weight percentage of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ was estimated about $82.8\%$ according to the thermogravimetric (TG) analysis in air (Figure S7). The high resolution TEM image (Figure 1b) clearly showed the $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ nanocrystallites were embedded in the amorphous carbon matrix, whose specific lattice spacing of {110} facet could be measured as $0.336\mathrm{nm}$ . Careful examinations on different areas confirmed the size of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ was typically smaller than $4\mathrm{nm}$ . The elemental mapping was employed to confirm the uniform distribution of Mo, O, and C through the whole octahedrons (Figure 1c). After phosphorization by $(\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{2}\mathrm{HPO_{4}}$ heated at $850^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{Ar}$ $(10\%)$ gas flow, the hybrid of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}@\mathbf{PC}$ was converted in situ into hexagonal $\mathbf{MoP@PC}$ , which was verified by XRD pattern in Figure 2a. The reaction that $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ underwent was believed to follow the equation: $2\mathbf{MoO}_{2}+2\mathbf{PH}_{3}+\mathbf{H}_{2}{\longrightarrow}2\mathbf{MoP}+4\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}.$ Carful check on TEM images revealed this phosphorization would not result in large MoP NPs that were more than $5\mathrm{nm}$ within the octahedral support (Figure S8). Moreover, this mild reaction would not destroy the well-defined octahedral morphology (Figure 1d and S9), demonstrating the process occurred in the cages of UIO-66. As clearly seen in Figure 1 e, a closer TEM examination locating at the edge of a MoP nano-octahedron gives more details of the lattice fringes of MoP NPs, which was consistent with the d spacing of the (100) planes. The EDS mapping collaboratively support the MoP NPs distributed homogeneously within the whole composites (Figure 1 f).
The BET specific surface area of the as-prepared samples were obtained by recording nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherms at $77\mathrm{K}$ (Figure 2b). The BET surface area of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}/\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}@\mathrm{C},\mathbf{MoO}_{2}@\mathrm{PC}$ , and $\mathbf{MoP@PC}$ were 132, 554, and $328\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, demonstrating the treatment by HF could effectively remove the inert $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and expand the reactive surface area. It was found nitrogen uptake exhibited a prominent increase at a relative low pressure $(P/P_{0}<0.01)$ , which indicated the inherent micropores of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}@\mathbf{PC}$ and $\mathbf{MoP@PC}$ . This result was consistent with the pore size distribution obtained from adsorption data calculated by BJH method (Figure 2b, inset), which showed a dominant pore with diameter centering at $1.8\mathrm{nm}$ for both of $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}@\mathbf{PC}$ and $\mathbf{MoP@PC}$ . It is worth mentioning there was no clear peak at the curve of pore size distribution for $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}/\mathbf{ZrO}_{2}@\mathbf{C}.$ . So, the pore centered at $1.8\mathrm{nm}$ was apparently derived from etching of the $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ nanoparticles by HF. In addition, the pore distribution and pore volume of $(0.341{\mathrm{~cm}}^{3}\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ $\mathbf{MoP@PC}$ remained unchanged after phosphorization at high temperature.
The $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was further used to characterize the valence state and composition evolution from $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}@\mathbf{PC}$ to $\mathbf{MoP@PC}$ . The survey spectrum (Figure S10) showed that $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}@\mathbf{PC}$ and $\mathbf{MoP@PC}$ were composed of Mo, O, C and Mo, P, C, respectively. Figure 2 c indicated four peaks with lower energy spin-spin doublet were attributed to the oxidation state of the ${\bf M}{\bf o}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ at 232.9 and $229.7\mathrm{eV}$ due to Mo $(3\mathrm{d}_{3/2})$ and Mo $(3\mathrm{d}_{5/2})$ , respectively. The peaks at 235.8 and $231.5\mathrm{eV}$ were assigned to the Mo $\left(3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}\right)$ and Mo $(3\mathrm{d}_{5/2})$ of $\mathbf{Mo}^{\mathrm{VI}}$ , respectively. The O1s peak can be split into three deconvoluted components. Two components at 531.5 and $533.2\mathrm{eV}$ were $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{O=C}}$ and $_{\mathrm{O-C}}$ derived from incomplete deoxidization of carboxyl. The third component at $530.5\mathrm{eV}$ arises from the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{O-Mo}}$ bonds (Figure 2 d).[14] Figure 2 e shows that two doublets at $235.6\:\mathrm{eV}/232.7\:\mathrm{eV}$ $(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{o}^{\mathrm{{VI}}}$ $\mathrm{3d}_{3/2}/3\mathrm{d}_{5/2})$ and $232.6\:\mathrm{eV}/229.2\:\mathrm{eV}$ $\mathrm{(Mo^{IV}~}3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}/3\mathrm{d}_{5/2})$ can be assigned to high oxidation state of Mo $\mathbf{\left(MoO_{3}\right)}$ and $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}\mathrm{;}$ ).[15] The rest of two double peaks could be ascribed to MoP, which was presented at $231.8\mathrm{eV}$ and $228.5\mathrm{eV.}^{[16]}$ Therefore, the surface of small MoP NPs in porous carbon was slightly oxidized, which was due to their high surface energy.[14] This oxidation of MoP was also observed in the XPS spectrum of P 2p. That is, the peak at $134.0\mathrm{eV}$ could be attributed to $\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}$ or $\mathbf{P}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{5}$ caused by slightly oxidized P (Figure 2 f).[17] Meanwhile, the doublet $130.4\:\mathrm{eV}/129.5\:\mathrm{eV},$ ) can be assigned to $\mathrm{{\bfP}^{-}}$ Mo species in $\mathbf{MoP@PC}$ .
With minor modification, this MOFs-assisted strategy to generate Mo-based composites could be readily generalized to other hybrids. For example, $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}$ and MoN nanoparticles were systematically synthesized under different atmosphere of $H_{2}/\mathrm{Ar}(10\%)$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}@\mathbf{PC}$ as precursor. As verified by XRD patterns, the $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}$ and MoN NPs confined in the mesoporous carbon both exhibited typical hexagonal phase after the transformation. (Figure S11). Furthermore, they both kept the initial porous structure and octahedral morphology. The EDS mapping confirmed the uniform distribution of C, N, Mo and C, Mo, respectively, in the whole nano-octahedral carbon (Figure S12). SEM images revealed the $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@\mathbf{PC}$ and $\mathbf{MoN@PC}$ both exhibited regular octahedral morphology and the average sizes were both $250\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S13). Moreover, XPS spectrums further confirm the successful phase evolution from $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ to $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}$ and MoN, respectively, whose details about valence state can be seen in the Figure S14.
Not only the nanocrystallization of MoP originated from the confinement of porous carbon, but also the extraordinary accessibility and stability endowed by the carbon shell could benefit the application of $\mathbf{MoP@PC}$ in electrocatalysis. The electrocatalytic activity of Mo-based composites including $\mathbf{MoP@PC}$ , $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@\mathbf{PC}$ , $\mathbf{MoN@PC}$ and $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}@\mathbf{PC}$ were further investigated towards HER. All the loading of electrocatalysts were set to $0.41\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ on the glassy carbon electrode and the electrocatalysis were all conducted in ${\bf N}_{2}$ - saturated $0.5\mathrm{{M}\ H_{2}\mathrm{{SO_{4}}}}$ solution with a scan rate of $50\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ . The representative polarization curves in Figure 3a indicated the bare porous carbon (PC) and $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}@\mathbf{PC}$ both exhibited poor HER activity. The $\mathbf{MoP@PC}$ was unexpectedly active for HER. As summarized in Table 1, $\mathbf{MoP@PC}$ possessed a lower onset potential of $77\mathrm{mV}$ compared to $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@\mathbf{PC}$ $(115\mathrm{mV})$ and MoN@PC $(258\mathrm{mV},$ ). As a comparison, we deliberately synthesized bulk MoP using commercial $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ and $(\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{2}\mathrm{HPO_{4}}$ as precursors under the atmosphere of Ar and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ $(10\%)$ at $850^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (see details in Supporting Information). Shown in Figure S15, the bulk MoP with hexagonal phase and irregular morphology had been successfully obtained. The current density at $200\mathrm{mV}$ of $\mathbf{MoP@PC}$ was higher than the $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@\mathbf{PC}$ , $\mathbf{MoN@PC}$ , $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}@\mathbf{PC}$ and bulk MoP, revealing its higher electrocatalytic activity. To achieve current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , the $\mathbf{MoP@PC}$ catalyst required smaller overpotential of $153\mathrm{mV}$ compared to $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@\mathbf{PC}$ $(177\mathrm{mV})$ , $\mathbf{MoN@PC}$ $(393~\mathrm{mV})$ and bulk MoP $(217\mathrm{mV})$ . To further study the HER activity, Figure $3\ensuremath{\mathrm{b}}$ clearly showed the Tafel plots which are calculated from polarization curves by Tafel equation. $\mathbf{MoP@PC}$ and bulk MoP exhibited the similar Tafel slope, which can be inferred the similar reaction mechanism of $\mathbf{MoP@PC}$ and bulk MoP, although the difference in the onset potential and inherent exchange current. In contrast, the Tafel slopes of $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@\mathbf{PC}$ and $\mathbf{MoN@PC}$ were $96\mathrm{mVdec}^{-1}$ and $167\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ , respectively, which were quite bigger than that of $\mathbf{MoP@PC}$ . Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurement was adopted to provide further insight into the electrical conductivity of electrocatalysts. $\mathbf{MoP@PC}$ showed a small value of charge transfer resistance $(26\mathrm{ohm})$ due to superior synergistic effect and stronger interaction between the nanosized MoP particles and porous carbon compared to that of $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@\mathbf{PC}$ (77 ohm) and bulk MoP ( $124{\mathrm{~ohm}}$ ; Figure 3 c). From Figure S16, the other electrocatalysts showed larger values of charge transfer resistance revealing low HER kinetics at its interface. To activity and stability for HER versus ${\bf M o}_{2}{\bf C}@{\bf P}{\bf C}.$ MoN@PC, $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}@\mathbf{PC}$ , and bulk MoP, benefiting from the outstanding size effect of small nanoparticles and highly exposed and accessible surface area. Our findings opened a new opportunities to synthesize other MOFs-based composites for various applications.
